s’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ0If thine enemy be hungry, give him bread to eat;¦layout ‚¤textÙ.And if he be thirsty, give him water to drink:¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ0For thou shalt heap coals of fire upon his head,¦layout ‚¤text¿And the LORD shall reward thee.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!The north wind driveth away rain:¦layout ‚¤textÙ1So doth an angry countenance a backbiting tongue.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ4It is better to dwell in the corner of the housetop,¦layout ‚¤textÙ/Than with a brawling woman and in a wide house.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!As cold waters to a thirsty soul,¦layout ‚¤textÙ#So is good news from a far country.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ.A righteous man falling down before the wicked¦layout ‚¤textÙ0Is as a troubled fountain, and a corrupt spring.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse…¤book§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses’„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines’‚¤textÙ!It is not good to eat much honey:¦layout ‚¤textÙ2So for men to search their own glory is not glory.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines’‚¤textÙ(He that hath no rule over his own spirit¦layout ‚¤textÙ6Is like a city that is broken down, and without walls.¦layout§quote_2ªlast_verse